{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>ואני</b> — It is possible that this is the meaning of the words of Asaph, or he speaks on behalf of every maskil, and that 'my feet' incline to turn from the straight paths, which are the paths of HaShem, in the manner of: 'my steps nearly slipped' (Psalm 73:2). For because the foot walks, therefore he likened the faith of the heart, which one habituates through walking, [to feet]. The word נטיו is complete, like: 'a perfect (תמים) rock' (Deut. 32:4). And behold, כאין — it would have been fitting for it to be like כמעט, and שפכו like נטיו, just as אשורי like רגלי, for the meaning is doubled according to the rule of eloquent language. But כאין we have not found except in the sense of 'nothingness' and that which is not. And by way of metaphor he referred to footsteps that were 'poured out like water,' in the manner of: 'I am poured out like water' (Ps. 22:15), because nothing remains in the vessel — 'my footsteps were poured out' and he has nothing with which to walk."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 73, :notes {0 "The Hebrew citation אשוריו / אשורי appears to refer to the same word in Ps. 73:2 (אשרוי / אשורי, 'my steps'); the text as transmitted contains some orthographic inconsistency between אשורים, אשוריו, and אשורי, which I have rendered uniformly as 'footsteps/steps.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:34:11.887734769Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}